{"id":1947,"date":"2020-12-14T09:15:31","date_gmt":"2020-12-14T17:15:31","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=1947"},"modified":"2024-09-01T13:56:50","modified_gmt":"2024-09-01T20:56:50","slug":"zumi-python-functions","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/zumi-python-functions\/","title":{"rendered":"1.4: Functions"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215130708a\" name=\"6a2215130708a\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Creating Custom Functions<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-size: medium;\">Functions are a way to save time and energy by storing commonly used blocks of code into one named set. You have been using Zumi\u2019s functions already by calling\u00a0<span style=\"font-family: Courier;\">zumi.forward()<\/span>\u00a0or\u00a0<span style=\"font-family: Courier;\">zumi.reverse()<\/span>. But did you know you can make your own functions?<\/span><\/p>\n<h3 id=\"Defining-functions\">Defining functions<\/h3>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: medium;\">The first step to writing your own function is the keyword\u00a0<span style=\"font-family: Courier;\">def<\/span>. You are letting Python know that you are about to\u00a0<strong>define<\/strong> a function. Next, provide your function name followed by parentheses and a colon. After the colon, indent and include the body of the function, which will contain what you want your function to do. For example, let&#8217;s create a function that greets you!<\/span><\/p>\n<p>In [ ]:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">def greet_user():\r\n    print(\"Hello\")<\/pre>\n<p>If you run the code above, nothing will happen. This code only defines the function but won&#8217;t run until you call it.<\/p>\n<p>In [ ]:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">greet_user() # Call the function<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221513071f3\" name=\"6a221513071f3\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Defining functions with parameters<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]What if we want the function to do something different depending on the parameters we provide? Remember that you can change the number of seconds Zumi drives forward in\u00a0<span style=\"font-family: Courier;\">zumi.forward()<\/span>. You can also add parameters to your functions!<\/p>\n<p>In [ ]:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">def greet_user(name):\r\n    print(\"Hello\", name)\r\n\r\ngreet_user(\"Zumi\")<\/pre>\n<p>We&#8217;ve modified the function definition to say &#8220;hello&#8221; to whichever name is provided as a parameter. In the example, we\u00a0<strong>pass<\/strong>\u00a0&#8220;Zumi&#8221; as a parameter to the function. Try calling the function again, but with a different name. Make sure it&#8217;s in quotation marks!<\/p>\n<p>In [ ]:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">greet_user(\"CHANGE ME!\")<\/pre>\n<p>What do you think would happen if there weren&#8217;t any quotation marks in the function call? Or if we put numbers?<\/p>\n<p>In [ ]:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">greet_user(12345)<\/pre>\n<p>Since we defined the function to print to the screen, any input needs to be in quotation marks. That does not mean you can&#8217;t write functions that can use numbers! Most of Zumi&#8217;s parameters, like speed and duration, are numbers.<\/p>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22151307266\" name=\"6a22151307266\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Multiple parameters<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Just like with\u00a0<span style=\"font-family: Courier;\">zumi.forward()<\/span>, you can make functions that require more than one parameter. The example below shows a function that requires two inputs and adds them together.<\/p>\n<p>In [ ]:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">def add(x,y):\r\n    print(x+y)<\/pre>\n<p>Try calling the function with the numbers 2 and 3 separated by a comma. What do you think will happen?[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221513072ca\" name=\"6a221513072ca\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Functions with Zumi<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]You have the basics down, so now try and create a function that uses drive commands. Here is an example that uses drive commands to make a square:<\/p>\n<p>In [ ]:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">from zumi.zumi import Zumi # Don't forget to import the zumi library!\r\nzumi = Zumi() # ...and create Zumi object!\r\n\r\ndef my_square(): # Function definition \r\n    zumi.forward() \r\n    zumi.turn_left()\r\n    zumi.forward()\r\n    zumi.turn_left()\r\n    zumi.forward()\r\n    zumi.turn_left()\r\n    zumi.forward()\r\n    zumi.turn_left()\r\n    \r\nmy_square() # Call the function with the parameter to change the square size<\/pre>\n<p>Make new functions below! If you want a challenge, try modifying the square function above to accept a seconds parameter to make your square bigger or smaller.[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 5 Creating Custom Functions [vc_column_text]Functions are a way to save time and energy by storing commonly used blocks of code into one named set. You have been using&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/zumi-python-functions\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":2050,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":false,"_lesson_complexity":"","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":810,"_lesson_preview":"preview"},"lesson-category":[],"lesson-tag":[2135,2131,2123,2122,2134,2230],"class_list":["post-1947","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-beginner","lesson-tag-functions","lesson-tag-fundamentals","lesson-tag-programming","lesson-tag-python","lesson-tag-zumi","module-beginner","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/1947","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/lesson"}],"version-history":[{"count":8,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/1947\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":9142,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/1947\/revisions\/9142"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media\/2050"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media?parent=1947"}],"wp:term":[{"taxonomy":"lesson-category","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-category?post=1947"},{"taxonomy":"lesson-tag","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-tag?post=1947"}],"curies":[{"name":"wp","href":"https:\/\/api.w.org\/{rel}","templated":true}]}}